{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgF8qHzkv2BUoiL7pnt5S","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"prompt\": \"A square, purely nonrepresentational abstract painting that fills the entire canvas edge to edge, with no frame, border, wall, room, or gallery setting. Heavy oil and cold wax on a rough surface. A dense, turbulent mass of deep ultramarine, blackened teal, and burnt umber fills the lower left and center. It is built from many translucent layers, scraped back so older colors show through in jagged seams. Short, fast, stabbing palette-knife strokes run against long, dragged brush sweeps, which creates tension and a sense of competing pulls. From the upper right, one broad, calm field of pale bone white and faint grey-lilac pushes into the turbulence. It is thinly glazed and almost empty, a deliberate area of quiet and open space. Where the two zones meet, a narrow, sharp seam of hot cadmium orange and a thread of acid yellow-green cuts diagonally across the canvas. The color there is raw and unblended, with a hard edge on one side and a soft smear on the other. Small flecks of vermilion sit buried under the dark layers like faint, uncertain pulses. The brushwork keeps a clear rhythm of dense, then sparse, then dense again. The texture is thick impasto ridges beside smooth, scraped planes, with visible drips, dry-brush drag, and sgraffito scratches. Contrast is strong between dark and light and between warm and cool, but the whole surface holds together as one gestural, emotionally charged composition. It should feel restless, searching, and honest, with a small area of calm at its edge. No recognizable objects, figures, faces, bodies, scenes, landscapes, horizons, machines, diagrams, symbols, icons, letters, numbers, or text.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":432,"cache_read_input_tokens"